Q: snprintf with unknown size of the char[] A: <p>At some point you must know how long your string will be.  Simple examples like this you can just choose a sufficiently big number.<br />
You're doing the right thing using snprintf if you happen to be wrong at some point.<br />
In cases where this isn't possible snprintf returns the number of characters it will use if the first two parameters are NULL and 0.  From there you malloc and free buffers determined at runtime.</p>
<pre><code>size_t size = snprintf(NULL, 0, ...) + 1;
char* str = malloc(size);
snprintf(str, size, ...);
//After doing what you want with the string
free(str);
</code></pre>
